   970                              <1> ; =============================================================================
   971                              <1> ;  Console layer
   972                              <1> ;
   973                              <1> ;  putc prints AL. waitk is the host's key routine: it waits up to EAX ms
   974                              <1> ;  (0: just a look; -1: for ever) for a key, and returns it in AL with the
   975                              <1> ;  zero flag clear, or AL = 0 with the zero flag set if none came. Both keep
   976                              <1> ;  every register but EAX (putc keeps EAX too).
   977                              <1> ;
   978                              <1> ;  A running program reads the keyboard only through getk and pollk, which
   979                              <1> ;  between them give the break key its rules:
   980                              <1> ;    - Ctrl-C stops the program;
   981                              <1> ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held at HELD for the
   982                              <1> ;      next '?', and a newer one takes its place;
   983                              <1> ;    - '?' (getk) takes the held key first;
   984                              <1> ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
   985                              <1> ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
   986                              <1> ;  Both are called straight from a handler (or from rpoll), never from a
   987                              <1> ;  routine under one: the checked build's report counts on finding one
   988                              <1> ;  return address above the data stack.
   989                              <1> ; =============================================================================
   990                              <1> 
   991                              <1> ; putc: WriteFile to the output handle (standard output; the checked
   992                              <1> ; report switches it to standard error), one byte. PUSHAD saves everything;
   993                              <1> ; the byte is AL in the saved EAX, and the count goes in the slot of the
   994                              <1> ; saved ESP, which POPAD ignores.
